# hilavitkutin-conformance Design Overview

`#![no_std]`, no alloc. The instruments a runner of work units is held to: a
one-thread reference runner that states what a frame means, a digest that
writes a frame's stores down as one word, and the laws, which are functions
over what runs wrote down. It depends on `hilavitkutin-api`, `arvo`,
`arvo-hash`, `notko` and `notko-hlist`, and on nothing of the engine.

## Purpose

The engine has three entries that run a frame, `run`, `run_parallel` and
`run_fused`, and a planner that is free to split the records into morsels,
spread them over cores, fuse units into one loop and rest a unit whose inputs
did not change. All of that is allowed only because none of it may change what
a frame computes. This crate holds the other half of that sentence: what a
frame computes, written once, in the plainest form there is, so that every
entry and every plan can be compared with it.

The reference runner takes value-form units through `RecordOp::apply` and
nothing else of them. It never calls `execute`, so it needs no context type and
no plan, and a unit written for the scheduler runs under it as it stands. It
keeps its stores in storage the caller lends, which is what keeps it free of an
allocator and lets the same code run on every target.

It is one crate and not a crate for each instrument. The digest produces the
words the laws compare and the runner produces the run they are compared with,
so nothing takes one of the three without the others, and a crate each would
put three entries in the layout for one kit.

## The frame's meaning

One frame, as the runner keeps it.

1. Every virtual is unfired and every accumulator is empty.
2. The units run in the sequence they are listed, each over every record of the
   count the runner was built with.
3. A unit with the schedule `On<V>` runs only when `V` was fired earlier in the
   frame. A unit with the schedule `Always` runs in every frame.
4. A read of a plain column sees what the column holds at that moment, so a unit
   listed behind a writer of the column reads what the writer wrote.
5. A read of a buffered column sees the frozen side, whatever the frame has
   written. A write to a buffered column lands on the live side.
6. An append lands behind the appends before it, so an accumulator holds its
   values in the sequence of the units and, inside a unit, of the records.
7. At the boundary, after the last unit, each buffered column that was written
   in the frame brings its live side in: by a copy under `Snapshot`, by trading
   the two sides under `Exchange`. A buffered column nobody wrote is left alone.

Point 3 is where the runner differs from the engine on purpose. The engine may
rest an `Always` unit under the incremental skip, and the engine's design says
that this is sound because a unit whose input cone did not change produces
identical output whether it runs or not. The runner rests nothing, so it is the
thing a skip has to equal.

A read with a reach is a `Window` over the same side an ordinary read of the
store sees, with the runner's record count as its extent.

## What the runner answers for

The meaning above is stated for a unit list in which every unit that fires a
virtual `V` stands ahead of every `On<V>` unit. The engine gives a virtual
fired behind its consumer a meaning across passes, and the runner does not
restate that. It refuses instead, with a value, and so does every other frame
it cannot keep.

| `Refusal` | When |
|---|---|
| `Short` | A lent column or side holds fewer records than the count the runner was built with. |
| `Full` | An append found the lent accumulator full. |
| `FiredBehind` | A unit fired a virtual after an `On` unit of that virtual had already been asked in the frame. |

A refusal ends the frame where it was met. The stores are left as they were at
that point and the runner is not meant to be used further.

## The runner's surface

`Oracle<W, K, V>` is the runner over a world `W`. A world is a nested pair of
`Lent` stores ending in `()`, one for each store the units name, found by type
the way the api finds a member of an access set. `K` is how the frame is kept
and defaults to `Faithful`. `V` is the sequence the records are visited in and
defaults to `Ascending`.

```rust
let world = (
    Lent::<Column<Pos>>::over(&mut pos),
    (Lent::<Buffered<Heat, Exchange>>::over(&mut frozen, &mut live), ()),
);
let mut oracle = Oracle::<_>::new(world, records)?;
oracle.frame(&(Drift, (Spread, ())))?;
let heat = oracle.of::<Buffered<Heat, Exchange>, _>().frozen();
```

`frame` is `units` followed by `boundary`. The two halves are public so that a
caller can read the stores between the last unit and the boundary, which is
what the isolation law is recorded from.

Each store kind has its lent form, and each form gives back what a frame left
in it.

| Store | Lent as | Read back |
|---|---|---|
| `Column<T>` | `Plain`, one slice | `values` |
| `Buffered<T, M>` | `Sides`, a frozen and a live slice | `frozen` |
| `Accum<T>` | `Events`, a slice and a count | `appended` |
| `Virtual<V>` | `Flag` | `fired` |
| `Resource<T>` | `Held`, one shared reference | nothing, a frame never writes it |

The traits under the surface are the type-level walk and are public because
the bounds of `frame` name them: `Kept` ties a store marker to its lent form,
`Find` finds a store in a world, `Reads` and `Writes` say what one member of a
read or write set does at one record, `Gather` and `Scatter` do that for a
whole set, `Due` says whether a schedule runs, `Units` walks a unit list, and
`Brings` is the boundary of one buffer mode.

## Visiting

`Ascending` visits record zero first and is the reference. `Descending` visits
the last record first. For every unit list the engine admits, the two leave the
same columns and the same flags behind, which is what the split law is checked
with on the runner itself. An accumulator's appends come out in the sequence of
the visiting, so a comparison across visitings records them sorted.

`Visiting` is sealed. A third sequence is a change to this crate.

## Mutants

A law that cannot fail holds nothing. `Keeping` is the trait `Faithful`
implements, and beside it stand the mutants: each keeps a frame wrongly in one
named way, and each is there to be run through the same laws and seen to fail.
They ship in the crate, under `oracle::mutant`, so that a consumer who holds a
runner of its own to the laws has the failing cases to hand as well.

| Mutant | What it gets wrong | Law it fails |
|---|---|---|
| `ReadsLive` | A read of a buffered column sees the live side. | model, isolation |
| `WritesFrozen` | A write to a buffered column lands on the frozen side, in place. | model, split, isolation |
| `BringsUnwritten` | The boundary brings in a live side nobody wrote. | model, isolation |
| `SkipsLast` | The last record visited is left out. | model |
| `KeepsAppends` | An accumulator is not emptied at the start of a frame. | model |
| `KeepsFlags` | A virtual stays fired across the boundary. | model |
| `RunsUntriggered` | An `On` unit runs whether its virtual fired or not. | model |

`Keeping` is sealed, and its members are constants a frame folds over, so the
faithful runner carries no branch a mutant would take.

## The digest

`Word` is 64 bits, `arvo::Bits<64, Hot>`, and is what every recorded run is made
of. `Fold` folds words into one: FNV-1a over 64 bits, from `arvo-hash`, over
each word's bytes least significant first, so neither the host's byte sequence
nor its pointer width enters. A digest of a frame is the fold of the frame's
recorded words, and a digest stream is one such word a frame.

How a store's values become words is the caller's to say, since the values are
the unit author's types. The crate asks only that the same stores are recorded
in the same sequence on both sides of a comparison.

## The laws

A law is a function over recorded words that returns a `Verdict`: `Holds`, or
`Fails` with the `Law` and a `Breach` that says where it first failed. A law
runs nothing. The caller runs, records and hands the records over, so a law
reads the same over any runner.

| Function | `Law` | It holds when |
|---|---|---|
| `model` | `Model` | The run's words equal the model's, in the same sequence. |
| `split` | `Split` | Every stream equals the first. The streams are runs that differ only in how the records were split and visited: the entry, the worker count, the morsel size, the visiting. |
| `isolation` | `Isolation` | The frozen sides before the boundary equal the frozen sides the frame began with, and the committed sides of runs that began with different live sides are equal. |

A `Breach` is `Differs` with the stream and the word, `Length` with the stream
where two records are not of one length, or `TooFew` where a law that compares
runs with each other was handed fewer than two. Streams are counted from zero:
for `model` stream zero is the run; for `split` the first stream is the
reference and the rest count from one; for `isolation` stream zero is the
frozen sides before the boundary and the poisoned runs count from one, the
first of them being the reference for the rest.

## What is not here

The crate holds no units and no model of any. The author units the engine is
held with, and the hand model beside them, are `hilavitkutin-frozen-authors`.
The comparison of the host's digest stream with another target's is a tool of
the repository, not of this crate.
